  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nPatchy fog early this morning could become locally dense at \ntimes in south central and southeastern North Dakota, though \nshould improve by mid to late morning. Light precipitation will \nlikely develop this morning and into the afternoon over south \ncentral North Dakota, including the James River Valley, as \nanother shortwave slides through the Dakotas. In this case, \nthermal profiles will remain plenty warm to ensure precipitation\nfalls in the form of rain. For the most part, rain should be \nlight, though a narrow corridor of a tenth or two of an inch is \npossible. As this system moves off to the east, remaining low to\nmid-level stratus will also clear off from west to east.\n\nOnce any precipitation ends in the south central today, mostly \ndry conditions are expected through Friday night as dry, quasi-\nzonal flow aloft prevails through Friday. During this \ntimeframe, an upper Pacific low off southwestern Canada will \ngradually churn towards the Pacific Northwest. By Friday night, \nflow aloft starts to become more southwesterly as this low \ngradually enters over the northwest CONUS and associated trough\nbegins to amplify. Come Saturday, low rain chances begin to \nenter the forecast. However, models are starting to favor a \nslower progression that holds off more widespread rain chances \nuntil Sunday night into early next week. Furthermore, a split \nflow pattern is becoming more favored to develop, which would \nlikely keep the heaviest precipitation south and possibly also \nnorth of the forecast area. Still, uncertainty remains in the\nevolution of this pattern, including what happens with a strong\nblocking high at the surface that will travel across the\nNorthern Plains to finish out the workweek, and then through \nthe Great Lakes Region and into the northeast CONUS this \nweekend.\n\nHigh temperatures for the remainder of the week and into early\nnext week are favored to become above average most days, for \nmost locations. This translates to highs frequently ranging from\nthe 40s north to 50s south to finish out the workweek, and \nmostly in the 50s everywhere this weekend. A few 60 degree \nreadings are also possible at times mainly in the southern half\nof the state. Seasonable lows mostly in the 20s to low 30s are \nexpected tonight through Thursday night, though will warm into \nthe 30s and 40s for most locations by Saturday night.",
